            <h3>High-Speed vs Regional Trains</h3>
            <p>Lincoln Service (Higher-Speed Service): This is the primary service on the route, operating at speeds up to 110 mph (177 km/h) on upgraded track segments between Joliet and Alton. It offers direct routes with consistent and predictable journey times, making it the best option for travelers prioritizing speed and convenience. </p>
            <p>Texas Eagle (Long-Distance): The Texas Eagle operates on the same tracks and serves Bloomington-Normal as part of its longer journey from Chicago to San Antonio and Los Angeles. It is slightly slower due to additional stops but offers the same direct service and connects to a broader national network. </p>
            <h3>What This Means for Travelers</h3>
            <p>If you choose the Lincoln Service, the journey feels efficient and straightforward. The higher-speed infrastructure on parts of this corridor makes it one of Amtrak's faster Midwest routes and has reduced travel time by approximately 30 minutes compared to previous schedules. The Texas Eagle option is relevant if you are combining this trip with travel to destinations further south like Springfield, St. Louis, or beyond. For direct Chicago-Bloomington travel, the Lincoln Service is the clear choice. This route is ideal for both short visits and longer stays due to its manageable duration. </p>
